---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const contrastHref = toolPathBySlug('color-contrast-checker');
const formatHref = toolPathBySlug('color-format-converter');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you build a color scale from one hex? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Paste one color. The page returns eleven stops, numbered 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600,
      700, 800, 900, and 950. Hue stays the hue you typed. Lightness walks from a near-white tint
      down to a near-black shade. Chroma, the amount of color, is kept when the screen can show
      it and reduced when it cannot. Under the row is a :root block of CSS variables, from
      --shade-50 to --shade-950.
    </p>
    <p>
      That block is the thing you came for. Copy CSS takes all of it. Tap a single swatch to copy
      only that hex. #3b82f6, rgb(59, 130, 246), hsl(217, 91%, 60%), and a name like teal all
      count as input. The arithmetic stays in the browser. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Color Shades Generator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="tints">
    <h2>How do tints and shades split the ramp? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A tint moves a color toward white. A shade moves it toward black. On this ramp the light
      stops are the tints and the dark stops are the shades. The stop whose lightness is closest
      to the color you typed holds your exact hex, so a mid blue lands on 500 and a pale yellow
      lands nearer 100 or 50. The other stops are lightness targets in the same hue.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 50 to 950 color palette contains eleven stops, named the way a Tailwind color scale names
      them, and tints versus shades is simply the light half against the dark half. The stops use
      those names because stylesheets already do. A token called
      --shade-700 reads as the dark step of the same family. You still have to look at the hex.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, try a familiar blue. Input: #3b82f6. Output: it lands on stop 500, and on white
      it is 3.68:1, under the 4.5 that body text needs. Stop 600, #1961d2, reaches 5.70:1, so it is
      the first stop in this ramp that can carry body text on white.
    </p>
    <p>
      The row is a grid that fills the width you have. On a phone the stops wrap. On a wide
      window they share one line of swatches instead of leaving a blank band beside three tiles.
      Each swatch is a button. The pressed state is the feedback, because a phone has no hover.
    </p>
    <p>
      A useful check is to type the same hue twice, once dark and once light. The dark one should
      put the brand stop lower on the ramp, closer to 800 or 900. The light one should put it
      up near 50 or 100. If both land on 500, the lightness walk is ignoring the color you typed.
      Here the nearest stop moves with the source, and the CSS names stay put so a stylesheet
      can keep referring to --shade-500 while you try another brand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="oklch">
    <h2>Why build the ramp in OKLCH? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      HSL calls yellow and blue the same lightness when one looks much lighter than the other.
      A ramp that steps HSL lightness by equal numbers goes dull in the middle and jumps at the
      ends. OKLCH, from Björn Ottosson's OKLab, steps lightness in a space where those steps look
      closer to even, which is why OKLCH ramps stay more even than HSL ramps of the same step size.
      Hue is an angle. Chroma is a distance from gray. This OKLCH palette generator holds the angle
      and walks the lightness, so the whole shade scale keeps one hue.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chroma is the part that breaks first. A lightness of 0.97 cannot carry the chroma of a
      strong blue and still land inside sRGB. The hex would have to clip a channel to 0 or 255,
      and the hue would drift. The page searches, by halves, for the largest chroma at that
      lightness that still fits. The stop gets duller. It does not change family. A gray input
      has chroma zero, so every stop stays gray and only lightness moves.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a color scale generator, not a clone of a published palette. Tailwind's stop names
      are the vocabulary. Tailwind's hex values are a hand-tuned set that changes between
      versions. Deriving eleven stops from your one color will not reproduce blue-500 from a
      docs page, and it should not. If you need the exact hex from a file, copy it from the file.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Hold hue, spend chroma">
      <p>
        Lightness is the ramp. Hue is the brand. Chroma is what you give up so the hex still
        means the color you see.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="contrast">
    <h2>When does the brand stop fail on white? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The stop nearest your color is checked as text on white. WCAG asks for 4.5:1 for body text.
      If the ratio is under that, a line under the row names the stop and the ratio. If the stop
      already clears 4.5, the line stays hidden. #3b82f6 is a useful demo: the stop near it fails,
      so the note appears. A deep navy such as #1e3a8a usually passes, and the note does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The failure mode is a swatch that looks fine in a row of its siblings. The row is not white
      body copy. Shipping --shade-500 as a text color because the tile was pretty is how a brand
      color becomes unreadable. The note is one sentence, and it is absent when there is nothing
      to warn about. It does not score stop 50, and it does not score colored backgrounds.
    </p>
    <p>
      For any other pair, open the <a href={contrastHref}>contrast checker</a> and type the two
      hex values. For the same color in HEX, RGB, HSL, and OKLCH at once, open the
      <a href={formatHref}>color format converter</a>. This page builds the ramp. Those pages
      answer a different question about one color.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting a Tailwind match">
      <p>
        Stop 500 from this page is built from your color, not Tailwind's blue-500. The names
        line up. The hex values do not, and they are not supposed to.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trusting a light brand for body text">
      <p>
        If the note names a ratio under 4.5, do not set that stop as text on white. Use a darker
        stop from the same ramp, then confirm the pair in the contrast checker.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Keeping chroma that the screen cannot show">
      <p>
        A pale stop that "keeps" a vivid chroma is clipping. The duller hex is the one that
        matches the swatch. Copy that one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Opaque tokens only">
      <p>
        Alpha is dropped. A shade ramp is a set of solid colors. If you need a translucent
        overlay, add the alpha in CSS after you paste the variable. A later color-mix()
        can tint one stop. The ramp itself stays eleven opaque hex colors, because a token
        file that hides alpha inside every step is harder to read than a solid scale plus
        one overlay where you use it.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Paste the variables, then check the pair you will actually ship in the
      <a href={contrastHref}>contrast checker</a>. If a teammate hands you hsl() and you want the
      hex before you build a ramp, start in the <a href={formatHref}>color format converter</a>
      and come back with one color.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rename --shade-500 if the project already uses that name. The later rule in the cascade
      wins, and a quiet overwrite is worse than a longer name. The ramp is a starting set of
      tokens, computed on your device, from the single color you typed.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Build a scale from #3b82f6 →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
